{#
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  THE CHOICE — the switch that /cookies promised and did not have
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  For years the only way to refuse arrival counting on this site was to send `DNT`
  or `Sec-GPC`. Chrome removed the Do Not Track setting and Safari removed it before
  that, so for most of this platform's visitors — Chrome on an Android phone — there
  was no way to say no at all, while the same page promised "you will be asked
  before it runs".

  So this is the part that has to work with nothing else: no JavaScript, no
  Alpine, no fetch. It is a plain form with two submit buttons and it posts. A
  privacy control that needs scripts to run is a privacy control that is missing
  for exactly the people most likely to have switched scripts off.

  ── WHY TWO BUTTONS AND NOT A TOGGLE ────────────────────────────────────────

  Because a toggle has to be read before it can be used — you have to work out
  which way is on — and because the accessible name of a switch changes meaning
  depending on its state, which is the one control type screen-reader users most
  often report having to test by flipping. Two buttons each say what pressing them
  does. The current state is stated above them in words, once, and the button for
  the state you are already in is not drawn: pressing it would do nothing, and a
  dead control is worse than an absent one.

  ── THE STATE IS SERVER-RENDERED ────────────────────────────────────────────

  `ag_privacy` is HttpOnly, so nothing here could read it even if it wanted to.
  That is deliberate: handing every page's scripts a privacy preference to read
  adds a fingerprinting surface to save a round trip we are not making.
#}
{% set c = cookie_control %}

<style nonce="{{ csp_nonce }}">
  /* ── ONE ROLE ON THIS PAGE, AND IT IS `action` ──────────────────────────────
     A privacy control that is loud reads as one that is selling something. The
     state is stated in words and set in the house ink; the single accent is on
     the button, because pressing it is the only thing this card asks for.
     Support\Accent, and AccentTest's ceiling of two roles per screen — this one
     spends one. The gold belongs on the hall of fame, not here. */
  .ck{ border:1px solid var(--ag-line-strong,rgba(16,41,44,.12)); background:#fff;
       border-radius:16px; padding:clamp(1.15rem,3vw,1.6rem); margin:0 0 clamp(1.8rem,4vw,2.4rem); }
  .ck__kicker{ margin:0 0 .85rem; font:600 10.5px/1 var(--ag-font-mono,monospace);
               letter-spacing:.16em; text-transform:uppercase; color:var(--ag-ink-soft,#626a6e); }

  .ck__state{ display:flex; align-items:baseline; gap:.55rem .8rem; flex-wrap:wrap; margin:0 0 .5rem; }
  .ck__now{ margin:0; font-family:var(--ag-font-display,Georgia,serif); font-weight:700;
            font-size:clamp(1.12rem,2.6vw,1.32rem); line-height:1.25; letter-spacing:-.015em;
            color:var(--ag-ink,#10292c); }
  /* Neutral, both ways. "We are counting your visits" is a state and not a warning; a
     red or amber chip here would dress an ordinary fact as a fault, and a green one
     would congratulate somebody for a choice they did not make. */
  .ck__chip{ flex:none; padding:.34rem .6rem; border-radius:999px;
             font:600 10px/1.3 var(--ag-font-mono,monospace); letter-spacing:.11em;
             text-transform:uppercase; white-space:nowrap;
             border:1px solid var(--ag-line-strong,rgba(16,41,44,.12));
             background:#f3f5f5; color:#3d4a4c; }
  .ck__chip--on{ background:var(--ag-ink,#10292c); border-color:var(--ag-ink,#10292c); color:#fff; }

  .ck__why{ margin:0 0 1.15rem; font-size:15px; line-height:1.62; color:#42504f; max-width:58ch; }

  .ck__act{ display:flex; gap:.65rem; flex-wrap:wrap; align-items:center;
            padding-top:1.1rem; border-top:1px solid var(--ag-line,rgba(16,41,44,.07)); }
  .ck__btn{ font:600 .94rem/1 inherit; padding:.7rem 1.15rem; border-radius:10px; cursor:pointer;
            border:1px solid var(--ag-action-edge); background:var(--ag-action-fill); color:#fff; }
  .ck__btn:hover{ filter:brightness(.92); }
  .ck__btn--quiet{ background:#fff; color:var(--ag-ink,#10292c);
                   border-color:var(--ag-line-strong,rgba(16,41,44,.12)); text-decoration:none;
                   display:inline-flex; align-items:center; }
  .ck__btn--quiet:hover{ background:#f3f5f5; filter:none; }
  /* 3:1 against both the white card and the green button it may sit on. */
  .ck__btn:focus-visible{ outline:3px solid var(--ag-ink,#10292c); outline-offset:3px; }

  .ck__note{ margin:1.05rem 0 0; font-size:13.5px; line-height:1.6;
             color:var(--ag-ink-soft,#626a6e); max-width:62ch; }
  .ck__note code{ font-size:.94em; }
  .ck__saved{ margin:0 0 .85rem; padding:.6rem .8rem; border-radius:10px;
              background:var(--ag-action-wash); border:1px solid var(--ag-action-edge);
              font:600 .9rem/1.45 inherit; color:#14331a; }

  @media (max-width:520px){
    .ck__act{ flex-direction:column; align-items:stretch; }
    .ck__btn{ text-align:center; justify-content:center; }
  }
</style>

<section class="ck" id="your-choice" aria-labelledby="ck-h">
  <p class="ck__kicker">Your choice</p>

  {% if c.saved %}
    {# Shown once, on the redirect after a press. A control that changes nothing you
       can see is a control people press twice. #}
    <p class="ck__saved" role="status">Saved. {{ c.saved_word }}</p>
  {% endif %}

  <div class="ck__state">
    <h2 class="ck__now" id="ck-h">{{ c.headline }}</h2>
    <span class="ck__chip ck__chip--{{ c.counting ? 'on' : 'off' }}">
      {{ c.counting ? 'Counting this visit' : 'Not counting' }}
    </span>
  </div>

  <p class="ck__why">{{ c.explanation }}</p>

  {% if c.locked %}
    {# Their browser said no. Drawing a "yes" button here would be offering to
       overrule them, and we have decided not to — so we say so instead of
       pretending the choice is open. #}
    <p class="ck__note">{{ c.locked_note }}</p>
  {% else %}
    <form method="post" action="/cookies/choice" class="ck__act">
      <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token }}">
      {% if c.counting %}
        <button type="submit" name="choice" value="no" class="ck__btn">Do not count my visits</button>
        <a class="ck__btn ck__btn--quiet" href="#counting-arrivals" style="text-decoration:none">What is counted?</a>
      {% else %}
        <button type="submit" name="choice" value="yes" class="ck__btn">Count my visits</button>
        <a class="ck__btn ck__btn--quiet" href="#counting-arrivals" style="text-decoration:none">What would be counted?</a>
      {% endif %}
    </form>
  {% endif %}

  <p class="ck__note">
    Remembering your answer needs one cookie of its own, <code>{{ c.cookie_name }}</code>. It holds a
    single character &mdash; a yes or a no &mdash; and no identifier of any kind. It is the record of
    your decision, so it is the one thing here we cannot offer to remove.
  </p>
</section>
